          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>William Wordsworth's Background</strong><br>\nWilliam Wordsworth was born in 1770 in Cockermouth, Cumberland, in the Lake District of England. His father was a legal representative for a prominent aristocrat, which afforded Wordsworth a quality education. After his parents' deaths during his childhood, he lived with relatives and developed a deep love for nature, which would heavily influence his poetry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Influence of Nature</strong><br>\nWordsworth attended Hawkshead Grammar School before going to St. John's College, Cambridge. Despite completing his degree, Wordsworth felt uninspired by the curriculum but enjoyed the natural surroundings of Cambridge. His long walks and appreciation for the landscape would become central to his later work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Publications and Initial Reception</strong><br>\nWordsworth's early poetry, including \"An Evening Walk\" and \"Descriptive Sketches,\" was published in 1793 to modest acclaim. These works show his burgeoning talent and reflect his personal experiences and impressions of nature. However, his early style lacked the distinct voice and innovative approach to nature that would characterize his later, more influential work.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
