          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Collaboration with Samuel Taylor Coleridge</strong><br>\nA significant turning point in Wordsworth's career was his collaboration with Samuel Taylor Coleridge. Together, they published \"Lyrical Ballads\" in 1798, which challenged the poetic norms of the day and is credited with launching the Romantic Age in English literature. Wordsworth's preface to the second edition outlined the group's revolutionary poetic theories.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of Romantic Poetry</strong><br>\nWordsworth's contributions to \"Lyrical Ballads\" displayed his mature style, emphasizing emotion and the individual's experience of the world. His belief that poetry should be written in the language of the common man was revolutionary, as was his focus on simple, unadorned diction and the beauty of the ordinary.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Rural Living</strong><br>\nWordsworth married Mary Hutchinson in 1802, and they had five children together. He spent most of his life in the Lake District, which continued to inspire his work. His poetry often reflected the belief that living a simple life in a natural environment was ideal for fostering the human spirit and creativity.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
