          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Wordsworth's Later Career</strong><br>\nWordsworth continued to write prolifically into his later years, and he was appointed Poet Laureate in 1843. His later works did not receive the critical praise that his early poems did. Critics believed that his appointment and the conservative turn of his later poetry conflicted with his earlier Romantic ideals.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophical and Spiritual Elements in Poetry</strong><br>\nWordsworth's poetry frequently delved into philosophy and the spiritual connection between humanity and nature. His autobiographical poem \"The Prelude,\" which he worked on throughout his life but was published posthumously, is an expansive work that details his spiritual journey and philosophical growth.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Enduring Legacy</strong><br>\nWordsworth died in 1850, having seen his reputation as a leading Romantic poet solidify. He left behind a vast oeuvre that continues to be celebrated for its innovative approach to poetic form and its profound meditations on nature, humanity, and the interplay between the two. His work remains influential and is a foundational part of English literature curricula worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
